00:00:25.068 --> 00:00:27.610
PROFESSOR: We're really lucky
to have a guest lecturer, David

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:32.890
Tames today, who is a very
talented and experienced

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.220
videographer and cinematographer
with a vast array

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.100
of experience, everything
from pioneering web

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.250
video through feature
films and in between.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.410
Also, he's a well-known tech
guru who has a well-regarded

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.540
blog, kino-eye.com.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.630
He's a graduate
of MIT's Media Lab

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.557
and currently an
instructor at MassArt.

00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:56.140
And he's going to
take us through some

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.270
of the basics of lighting.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.100
And we're then going
to do a demonstration

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.150
of basic interview
three point lighting,

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.470
which should be a lot of fun.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.932
So take it away, David.

00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:06.140
DAVID TAMES: Glad to be here.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.510
Thanks, Chris.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:11.140
So as Chris said, we're going
to talk about basic lighting.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.000
And we're going to use a simple
straightforward interview

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.190
as the subject of our
lighting discussion.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.040
Today, of course, there's a lot
of things that we can light.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:29.800
But basically, interview
lighting will be our focus.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.050
Just since we're at MIT, we
need to talk a little bit

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.030
about what light is.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.730
It's a tiny sliver of the
electromagnetic spectrum

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.460
that our eyes are sensitive to.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.440
As the wavelengths
get shorter, you

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.620
end up with things like
X-rays and gamma rays.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.390
And as the wavelengths
get longer,

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.980
we end up with radio waves, just
like this wireless mic going on

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.030
here.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.630
But this tiny, tiny, tiny sliver
of the electromagnetic spectrum

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.780
is what our eyes happen
to be sensitive to.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.300
And this is the medium
that we are working with.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:07.270
These are our materials that
we work with in film and video.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.789
And of course, there's
that classic experiment

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:11.500
that Newton did in his--

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.510
I guess it was his
grandmother's barn or something.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.000
Not only did he--

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.060
it was well known
at his time that you

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.720
could break white light
into the constituency

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.420
of the various colors
of the rainbow.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.630
But he also showed that once you
broke it down to those colors,

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.580
you couldn't break
it down any further

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.120
and that with a second prism,
you could recombine that light

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.230
back into white light.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.070
So it turns out that working
with any combination of red,

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.550
green, and blue light, we
can create any of the colors

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.900
that we're sensitive to.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.860
And so interestingly enough,
most of our imaging systems

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.850
today basically work with red,
green, and blue receptors,

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.820
which we will talk about
why red, green, and blue

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:58.930
happens to be the star colors
from which all other colors can

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.010
be derived.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.980
And it turns out that our
eyes have receptors sensitive

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.790
in the blue range, the green
range, and the red range.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.920
And we are most sensitive
to those in the green range.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.780
Perhaps our ancestors
who could see green

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.390
were more successful
than our ancestors

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.995
who were primarily sensitive
to other frequencies of light.

00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:22.870
Who knows about that.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.820
But enough technical stuff.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.740
When we talk about
light creatively,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.130
we're not thinking
about wavelengths

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.310
and the individual
colors that much,

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:35.330
but we're thinking about
certain characteristics.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.650
We often talk about intensity,
how bright is the light.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.580
And it turns out that since
most light sources radiate

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.780
in all directions, there's very,
very rapid falloff of light.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.732
So we call that the
inverse square law.

00:03:48.732 --> 00:03:51.190
So if you're by a window and
you have a really, really nice

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.200
exposure, walking
away from the window

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.590
just a little bit is enough
to end up in darkness.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:58.713
Because if you
double the distance

00:03:58.713 --> 00:04:00.130
between the light
and the subject,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.500
you only have one fourth
of the intensity as before.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:08.620
So you're always working
against the inverse square law

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.040
in terms of the intensity.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.100
So if you need your
light to be brighter,

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.530
you can always move it
closer to your subject

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.540
or get a bigger light.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.399
We also talk about
the quality of light,

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:20.500
whether it's hard or soft.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.870
Hard light has bold
shadows, crisp textures.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.300
We'll look at an example
in just a moment.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.460
And soft light has
a gentle contrast.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.728
It's more diffused, typically
very soft or no shadows.

00:04:31.728 --> 00:04:33.520
We often talk about
the direction of light.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.260
Where is it coming from?

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.940
It refers to the
path of the light

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.400
from the source of the
light to our subject.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.490
Is it coming from the
front, the side, the back,

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.840
under light, top light?

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.340
And we use these
terms to describe

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.500
where the light comes from.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.520
And of course, color, like we
just saw in the previous slide,

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.980
it's the relative
mix of wavelengths.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:56.800
And color is often motivated
by the light source.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.060
So if we want to
depict a sunny day,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.600
we use yellows and very,
very light blues and white.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.840
It can establish mood.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.450
So if we're going
to-- typically,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.850
period pieces that take
places during the Renaissance

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:13.555
happen to have an
orange glow about them.

00:05:13.555 --> 00:05:15.970
Just last night I
saw Goya's Ghost

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:20.860
and was reminded of how we seem
to depict certain time periods

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.110
with certain color palettes.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.170
Perhaps since the primary light
source during the Renaissance

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:30.850
was candles instead of HMI
daylight balanced lights

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:35.200
like we have on film sets
today, it makes a lot of sense

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.900
that in the evenings, the
world was predominantly

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.840
this golden yellow.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.390
PROFESSOR: Just want to add
one quick thing about that.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.480
As a documentary
filmmaker who's often

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.640
working in available
light, just remember

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.120
that those qualities that
David just talked about

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.250
apply to available light
just as much as to lights

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.310
that you might set up.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.247
So if you think about the
lighting in this room,

00:05:58.247 --> 00:05:59.830
it's really important
to start getting

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.670
sensitive to those
exact same situations.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:03.920
Where's the light coming from?

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.350
It's coming from
overhead lights.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.570
So what does that mean
for the way things look?

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.070
What are the colors
of those lights?

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:10.180
Well, they're fluorescents.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.770
But if you actually
look at the bulbs,

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.370
you see that they don't even
match from fixture to fixture.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:16.120
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:17.260
Some of these over
here are yellow

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.870
and some of these over here are
more towards the white range.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:21.252
PROFESSOR: Blue-white, yeah.

00:06:21.252 --> 00:06:22.960
So those are the kinds
of things that you

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.832
start to become sensitive to,
even as a documentary filmmaker

00:06:26.832 --> 00:06:27.790
working without lights.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.632
And all of those
things, the harshness,

00:06:29.632 --> 00:06:31.840
the quality of the light,
the intensity of the light,

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.960
the direction, the
color, are assessments

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.840
that you need to make even in
situations where you're not

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.340
actually controlling the light
with artificial light producing

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.680
instruments, like we're
going to work with later.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:45.430
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.320
I mean, even when I
go to do an interview

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.000
and I'm doing a pre-interview
and talking to the subject,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.480
I always say, can we talk
while we're walking around?

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.750
Because I want to look at
the light in the environment

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.910
and try to start with
something natural.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:06:58.870
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.040
DAVID TAMES: I mean,
just this weekend, we

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.915
were on Martha's Vineyard
doing an interview,

00:07:01.915 --> 00:07:05.650
and we carted the lighting
kit in the trunk of the car.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.270
We carried it around.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:08.740
We took it across the ferry.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:10.750
We carried this big,
huge lighting kit with us

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.670
wherever we went,
and I never cracked

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:17.368
it open because we were able
to find several positions to do

00:07:17.368 --> 00:07:18.910
our interviews where
the lighting was

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.420
perfect and natural.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.300
However, it was very
important for me

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:27.880
to know that if we didn't
find a beautiful spot,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.660
we could accent what
we had and supplement

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.197
with additional light.

00:07:33.197 --> 00:07:35.530
PROFESSOR: So one of the big
takeaways from this lecture

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:38.320
today isn't just how
to set up lights,

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:39.640
although that's very important.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.010
But it's also how to assess
the light that you have

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.590
and how to make choices
about where you shoot things

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:45.760
based on that assessment.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.635
DAVID TAMES: That's a
very important insight.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:56.890
So very briefly-- and by the
way, a PDF with the slides

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:00.880
and a PDF with written notes
to go with each of the slides

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.340
will be available as
part of this package.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.940
So I don't want to go
into too much detail here,

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.690
but there are two primary
light models that we use

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.700
when we're talking about light.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.290
We often talk about
the additive model.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:18.190
So I don't if you've been
to the Museum of Science,

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.850
but they have this
great exhibit where

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:24.320
they show three projectors,
blue, red, and green light.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.510
And where those three projected
light beams intersect,

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.550
you end up with
white light, which

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.662
I remember being
around five years old

00:08:33.662 --> 00:08:35.120
and seeing this
for the first time,

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.100
and I just thought
that was miraculous.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.830
Like how can those three
colors become white?

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:42.530
Because my experience with
paints was so different.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:44.600
But that's because my
real world experience

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.700
was the other lighting model,
which we'll get to in a moment.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.600
But that was magical that you
could combine these colors.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:55.250
It turns out if you combine red
light and its complement cyan,

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.200
you end up with
white light as well.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.300
Because if you have equal
amounts of red, green,

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.910
and blue light, then we
see what we call white.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:09.440
And so I would suggest
visiting the PDF documents

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.970
for the gory details.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.010
Now, when we're
working with-- often,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.380
we want to change the
color of a light source.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.980
And we'll put something
called a gel over that light.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.290
And when we're doing
that, we're using

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.030
what we call the
subtractive light model.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:25.730
In other words,
we're filtering out

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.380
some of the wavelengths
and not others.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:32.908
So it turns out that if you put
yellow, cyan, and magenta gels,

00:09:32.908 --> 00:09:34.950
which happen to be the
complements of red, green,

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.660
and blue, but if you put those
three gels over a light table,

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.850
for example, you will
see that you end up

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:44.738
with this muddy brown
darkness where they overlap

00:09:44.738 --> 00:09:46.280
because you have
pretty much filtered

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.580
out all of the light sources.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.340
They're not perfect filters,
so that black will never

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:53.570
be totally black.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:57.020
This is why in four color
printing of magazines

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:00.720
and newspapers, they use
cyan, magenta, yellow,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.990
and black ink for
that extra blackness.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:06.110
So that also uses the
subtractive system.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.060
So you can either
use pigments or you

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.850
can use gels to subtract
particular wavelengths.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.360
So for example, if my scene
was a little too orangey

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.320
and I wanted my light sources
to be a little less orangey,

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.080
I would be putting different
gradations of blue gels

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.890
over those lights, for example.

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.470
So that's the subtractive model.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:29.480
And once you get to know
this, it makes perfect sense.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.870
And we'll talk a little
more about blue and orangey

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.420
correction gels, which
we have a tendency

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:39.140
to use a lot in film video work,
because we're often correcting

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.050
or changing the light
from the cool blues

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:44.120
to the warmer orangey lights.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:48.300
But that's the
subtractive model at work.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.950
So it turns out
that video cameras

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:55.100
being technical devices,
they don't really

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.570
know what white is.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:00.050
Some combination of red, green,
and blue wavelengths is white.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:02.480
But your white might be
different than my white,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.240
might be different than
that Sony camera's white.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:08.750
So what we typically do when
we white balance the camera

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:11.450
is we point the camera
and hold up a white card.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.130
And we push the
white balance button.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.890
And we're telling the camera,
that particular combination

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:18.200
of red, green, and blue,
whatever it happens to be,

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.640
that's my white reference.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.790
And now the camera
will display whatever

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:25.820
that card is as pure white.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:31.190
Well, it turns out that
this relative balance

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.410
of red, green, and
blue, the camera

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.630
sees it as a technical
problem to solve.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.200
And very often, the perfect
white balance of a camera

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:45.680
is often maybe a little too
clean, too dry, too perfect.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:47.810
So a trick that I do
is I get something

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:52.160
called an eighth blue gel, which
is like a very pale blue gel.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:53.840
And when I white
balance my camera,

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:56.510
I hold that pale blue
gel over the camera lens,

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.190
I press the white
balance button,

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.660
and I push away the gel.

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:01.945
And what have I done?

00:12:01.945 --> 00:12:04.070
I have fooled the camera
into thinking that there's

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.240
more blue in the world.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.480
And it compensates by
making the image a teeny bit

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.420
warmer, a little more orangey.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:14.990
And flesh tones
look a lot better

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.040
with just a little
bit of warmth added.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.580
So this is my
little bit of basil

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:24.740
in the sauce for a
slightly tastier image.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.960
You will hear a lot of people
talk about color temperature.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:32.690
This is a method that
is used to describe what

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.520
the color of a light source is.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:39.800
We work so often in this orange
to blue to white dimension

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.950
simply because so many of our
artificial and natural light

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:45.500
sources are in that range.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:48.410
For example, fire,
candle, matches,

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:49.940
they're all kind of orange.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:53.030
Sunlight is very blue.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:59.600
And so it turns out that color
temperature is basically,

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.790
what temperature do
you need to heat up

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:04.700
this theoretical black
body radiator in order

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:05.810
to get that color?

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:09.260
Well, it turns out in
practical everyday world,

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.120
that black body
radiator that you've

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.580
read in cinematography
books, the equivalent,

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.820
think of it as a tungsten
filament, as a light filament.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.130
The more juice you put through
that filament, the less orangey

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.110
it is and the more
white it becomes.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.750
Of course, at some point,
it explodes because it's

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.040
handling too much current.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:31.230
But in theory, if you heated
it up to 9,000 degrees,

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:34.450
you would have the same color
as hazy sunlight, which is very,

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:36.670
very pale blue, almost white.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:41.160
But if you bring the temperature
down to 1,700 degrees Kelvin,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.280
you would have the nice,
orangey color of a match flame.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:46.740
So this is this
temperature scale

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:49.830
from orangey on the
low temperature end

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.710
to almost perfect white
with a slight tinge

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.660
of blue on the high end.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.160
There are two magic
numbers that you

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.160
hear in the world of white
balance, 3,200 degrees

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:02.880
Kelvin, which is the industry
standard for tungsten filament

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:04.860
incandescent lights.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:08.700
It is the standard that for
almost 100 years, well, maybe

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:13.170
80 years, film emulsions
were matched to that color

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.710
temperature, so that something
lit under 3,200 degree

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.290
Kelvin light would look white.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:20.910
There's also daylight
balanced films.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.910
So consumer film
was usually balanced

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:26.190
to 5,500 degrees Kelvin.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:27.660
And that's called
daylight balance.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:32.010
So most electronic flash
is balanced for daylight,

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:37.710
and consumer film was always
mostly balanced for daylight.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.850
And of course, now in the error
of video and digital imaging,

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:44.700
it's a little bit of a holdover.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:49.380
But we still use these terms
because film lighting still

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:52.230
comes primarily in
two flavors, tungsten

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:56.590
balanced, quartz halogen lamps,
LED lamps, fluorescent lamps,

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:57.090
et cetera.

00:14:57.090 --> 00:15:00.690
They're all available matched
perfectly 3,200 degrees Kelvin.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:04.320
And a new generation of HMI
daylight balanced lights,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.170
daylight balanced fluorescents,
daylight balanced LEDs,

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:08.970
they're available
matched perfectly

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:11.280
to 5,500 degrees Kelvin.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:15.240
PROFESSOR: And also many
cameras that you may use have

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.490
preset white balance settings.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.840
So there may be
one for daylight.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:21.090
There may be one for tungsten.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.580
And when you click it
to one of those settings

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.320
rather than create
the white balance

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:26.970
yourself, as David
just described,

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.928
essentially what you're
doing is the equivalent

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:32.340
of loading your camera with
an emulsion that's balanced

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:34.260
to one of those presets.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.310
So 3,200 is, in theory,
perfectly balanced

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:39.510
for tungsten light.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:42.660
And the daylight balance is
perfectly balanced, in theory,

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:45.492
for about 5,500, 5,600.

00:15:45.492 --> 00:15:46.950
DAVID TAMES: And
it's handy, right?

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:48.367
When you buy
lighting instruments,

00:15:48.367 --> 00:15:52.710
it's nice that they all
match for the most part.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.630
Now, if we look at these
three little pictures

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:58.290
here, something to think
about, outdoor shadows

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.990
are predominantly blue.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:06.270
If you shoot in shadow, you get
really, really bluish images.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:09.150
To our eyes, it may
look perfectly neutral.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.010
But to the camera,
it looks really blue.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:11.790
Why?

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.370
Because shadows don't
have any direct sun.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.440
All the lighting
in the shadow is

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.840
coming from the sky,
which is predominantly

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:22.740
blue due to the way that the
light waves are scattered.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:25.290
The different wavelengths are
scattered a different amount.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.380
And so the sky has a
tendency to be very blue.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.290
In the middle picture, you
see here a mixed environment.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.450
The interior lighting
in this museum

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:35.670
is tungsten
incandescent lighting,

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.390
but you have blue daylight
streaming through the skylight.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.240
These are the most difficult
situations to shoot,

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.430
because you basically have
to split the difference

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:47.190
or find some happy medium
between the tungsten

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.100
lighting and the daylight
lighting because there's no way

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:51.960
to reproduce that
the way the eye

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:55.410
sees it unless you do some
trickery in post-production.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:57.660
So often, we're
splitting the difference

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.960
between tungsten interior
lighting and daylight

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.610
streaming through windows.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:07.319
On a big Hollywood
budget, you'd send a crew

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:11.010
to maybe gel those windows and
put orange gel over the windows

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:14.849
so that now everything
is perfectly balanced.

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:16.560
As documentary
makers, we typically

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.930
don't have a crew that can gel
windows and things like that.

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:23.069
And so in some ways, we have to
be much more creative in terms

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:24.960
of how we choose
where we're going

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:28.840
to set the white
balance for our scene.

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.050
And of course, sunsets
are extremely warm due

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:32.760
to the scattering,
again, the way

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.260
the light rays are scattered.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.900
And they have a tendency
to be very, very warm,

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:39.810
very, very orangey.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.760
And the more crap that
we put in the atmosphere,

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:44.820
the more gorgeous
sunsets we get,

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:48.150
which is the one thing I do miss
about living in Los Angeles.

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.660
We had intense,
beautiful orange sunsets.

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:55.640
So the smog is
good for something.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:57.800
Let me relate this to
the color correction

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:00.020
that you're going
to see in post.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.630
We talked about the
green-cyan dimension

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:06.048
and the green-magenta
dimension and the blue-yellow,

00:18:06.048 --> 00:18:08.340
which is the dimension that
we're usually working with.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:11.330
And when you're correcting
images in post-production,

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:17.030
it turns out that
the same dimensions

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:20.970
that we saw in the
additive color model

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.923
come to work here as well
because our LCD monitors

00:18:24.923 --> 00:18:26.840
we're looking at, red,
green, and blue pixels.

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:30.050
And with our cameras, we have
red, green, and blue sensitive

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:30.930
sensors.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.710
So if our scene is a little too
yellow or a little too blue,

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.810
we can simply adjust in
our color correction dial.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.730
So if you can correct in
post-production these problems

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.280
with white balance, why do
we worry so much about it

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:44.870
while we're shooting?

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:49.040
The reason is you want
to get the white balance

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:53.090
right the first time when you
shoot because if the scene is

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:56.000
excessively blue or the
scene is excessively orange,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.150
what happens is you're going
to overexpose the red channel

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:02.690
or overexpose the blue channel.

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:06.740
And what happens is once you
overexpose that color channel,

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:10.370
there's no information or detail
to bring back when you correct.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.870
So you will always notice
that a properly white balance

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:14.870
scene, you can
fine tune the color

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.250
and get a very
rich, sensual image.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.107
But if you shoot the
wrong white balance

00:19:19.107 --> 00:19:20.690
and you go in to
correct, you're going

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:23.240
to notice that your
detail looks different

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:26.300
and your highlights don't have
the same texture and detail.

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:30.170
Because if you shot, for
example, daylight balanced

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.360
in a tungsten environment,
you have completely

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:37.950
whacked out the red channel
relative to the green and blue

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:39.420
channels.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:40.950
So you do want to get it right.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.490
Because having one
channel way overexposed,

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:46.360
that detail is lost forever.

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:52.110
So yes, you can correct in post,
but get at least a good middle

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:54.810
of the road starting
point in production.

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:58.380
I want to revisit
quality of light.

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:03.120
So on the left, we have a scene
that was shot on a cloudy day,

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.400
and it's raining.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.280
And by the way, a little
trick, on a cloudy day,

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.810
you would never see
the raindrops on film.

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:15.630
So we cheated, and we put
a very bright HMI daylight

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:18.390
balanced backlight
behind the actors

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:21.480
so that you would
see the raindrops.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:25.200
Otherwise, the lighting is
completely flat and diffused.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:30.900
But we cheated by having
a little hard backlight

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:32.250
that you don't actually see.

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.170
And then the raindrops show up.

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:37.440
But a typical overcast day
has very, very soft light.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:40.090
The light is coming from many,
many different directions.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:41.760
And so you don't see
distinct shadows,

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.390
and it has a
particular maybe soft

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:48.480
or maybe even a
gloomy feel, depending

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:52.050
on the context of the scene.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:55.200
Notice on the right
with direct sunlight,

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.800
you get very, very hard,
crisp, distinct shadows.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:01.350
So it turns out
that what determines

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:04.440
the quality of hard
light versus soft light

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:07.570
has a lot to do with the
size of the light source.

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:10.140
So even though the sun is many
times larger than the Earth

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:13.680
because it's so far away, it's
effectively a point source.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:16.380
And a teeny tiny point
source is going to give us

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.690
crisp, clean shadows.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:22.230
Whereas on an overcast
day, the entire cloud cover

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:26.160
is like Mother Nature's
beautiful silk.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:30.040
It's all light coming from all
sorts of different directions.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:31.860
And it's soft.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:35.490
And you can't beat the
beautiful quality of light

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:36.300
on a cloudy day.

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.000
And then you can add
a small accent light

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.130
for a little bit of
dimension, and you'll

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:42.780
get beautiful video images.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.180
But harsh sunlight
is pretty hard

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.280
to deal with,
especially with faces.

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:50.430
But we'll talk about it when
we do the hands on demo.

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:52.680
We'll talk about how we
can deal with those issues

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:55.420
in bright, direct sunlight.

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:58.020
So this is a summary
in more detail

00:21:58.020 --> 00:21:59.400
of what I've just talked about.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:03.240
But I want to point out that
in terms of soft light sources,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:06.120
we're looking at things
like soft boxes, which

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:11.310
have a large diffusion material
in front of a hard light

00:22:11.310 --> 00:22:14.070
or light bouncing off
of a white surface.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:17.550
This is how you create soft
light because you can't always

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:20.160
order in a cloudy
day and cloudy days

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:22.260
don't always walk into
the building with you.

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:24.690
So you may have to
create that soft light

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:27.660
with an artificial unit.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:32.730
Hard light, typically direct
sunlight, a Fresnel instrument,

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:35.760
any kind of open face
light with a reflector,

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:37.830
is going to give you hard light.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.280
What's nice, though, is you
can always soften a hard light

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.860
by throwing diffusion
material in front of it, which

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:45.310
spreads the beam.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.970
So it's always great to
have hard lights in your kit

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:49.440
because you can
always soften them.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:52.650
But once a soft light unit,
there's really no practical way

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.840
to make it hard again.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.900
Now, something that--
we are entering

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.560
this world of 3D cinematography
and all of that great stuff.

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:07.350
But it turns out that
for a zillion years,

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:11.550
photographers, painters,
cinematographers,

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:15.240
have been creating a
sense of three dimensions

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.270
without two cameras
and two lenses

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.260
but using light to do it.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:23.850
And the trick is to create
these alternating areas

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.970
of light and dark and
light and dark in a scene.

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:32.190
And the great prince of
chiaroscuro was Caravaggio.

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:34.500
Caravaggio basically
introduced black

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:38.520
into the painter's palette and
created these really dramatic

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.490
three dimensional effects
by alternating light

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.740
and dark and light and dark.

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:45.360
And we often talk
about Rembrandt

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.010
lighting in portraiture.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:54.000
And that little triangle in the
cheek opposite the light source

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.205
is one of the trademarks
of Rembrandt lighting.

00:23:58.205 --> 00:23:59.830
And it's not really
about the triangle.

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:01.800
But what the triangle
means is that,

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:03.360
once again, we've
created the sense

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.720
of light on the side of
the face and then darkness

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:09.180
behind the nose and then
another area of light

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:10.760
and then another
area of darkness.

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:15.090
And those alternating
areas of light and dark

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.800
create this sense of dimension.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:20.880
So by simply moving the
direction and the quality

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:22.800
of your primary
light source, you

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:26.190
can go from flat daytime
soap opera lighting

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:29.940
to dimensional dramatic
front line interview

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:33.040
or Rembrandt portrait lighting.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.310
And it's all about
the quality of light

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:37.430
and what direction
it's coming from.

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:41.660
And Rembrandt and Caravaggio
offer a wonderful study

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:45.980
in classic chiaroscuro lighting.

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:52.160
So let's talk about a few
practical, real world, almost

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:55.700
run and gun but not quite,
carrying three lights

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:57.920
interviews.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:03.350
So here's a frame from a film.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:06.140
The lighting is a little more
dramatic than you usually see.

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:10.190
I have a tendency to err
on the side of drama.

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:13.010
We sort of have that
little Rembrandt triangle

00:25:13.010 --> 00:25:14.360
on her cheek.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:16.100
And we can see that
there's alternating

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:21.090
areas of light, dark,
light, dark in this frame.

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:22.610
So how do we create this?

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:23.540
What did we do?

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:26.150
So if we look behind
the scenes, we

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:31.490
see that we have a primary
light source, a soft box.

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.230
The primary light
source is typically

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:35.870
called the key light,
our primary light source.

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:37.250
It's off to the side.

00:25:37.250 --> 00:25:39.500
It's creating that
sense of dimension.

00:25:42.590 --> 00:25:45.800
To the right, we have what's
called the fill light.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:49.040
Whatever light is filling
in the shadows created

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.650
by the key light, we
call that the fill light.

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:56.900
And in the case of this example,
we're using a reflector.

00:25:56.900 --> 00:25:59.390
It's called a Flexfill, which
you'll see in the live demo

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:01.980
portion of our show.

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:04.760
We're using a Flexfill to
reflect some of that key light

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:07.820
back onto the subject so that
the dark side of the face

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:10.700
doesn't become totally dark.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:14.120
And then slightly off the
top of the frame there,

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:15.500
we have a backlight.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:18.650
And we're using the backlight
to create a little bit-- if we

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:21.140
go back to this image,
there's a little bit

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:23.600
of light on her shoulder.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:26.510
And there's a little
bit of light on her hair

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:28.160
that's coming from behind her.

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:31.340
So what we did is we
have a 300 watt Fresnel--

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:35.180
we'll talk a little more
about Fresnels later--

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:37.850
behind her that's just
giving a little bit

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:39.590
of light coming
from the back, which

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:42.080
creates a little bit of
additional dimensionality

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:43.560
and separation from
the background.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.710
But in this particular
frame, it's very subtle.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:48.110
If you overdo the
backlight, all of a sudden,

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:50.300
things start to look theatrical.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:51.560
We're in the middle of a play.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:53.895
We're not in the middle
of a documentary anymore.

00:26:53.895 --> 00:26:56.270
So you have to be careful
because the backlight is a very

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:58.470
artificial element.

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.520
But if done subtly,
it can look very much

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:05.420
like a natural element and give
a little bit of separation.

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:08.660
And we can see a little
bit of shine in the hair.

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:11.510
And then we have a fourth light.

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:14.450
Because remember, we've got
the key light, the fill light,

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.063
filling in the shadows caused
by the key light, a backlight

00:27:18.063 --> 00:27:19.730
coming from behind
the subject, offering

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:20.813
a little bit of dimension.

00:27:20.813 --> 00:27:23.340
And there's a fourth light
that we often talk about,

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.430
and that's the background
light, not to be

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:26.840
confused with the backlight.

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:29.120
Now, the background light
in this particular scene

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:32.940
is being provided by the window
in the upper right hand corner.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:37.520
I've covered up part of
the window with foam core,

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:39.500
but I've left the
rest of the window

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:43.080
open to add this overall light.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:43.680
So if we go--

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:44.180
oops.

00:27:44.180 --> 00:27:48.680
If we go back here, the
overall ambient light

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:50.780
that's lighting the
back wall is a mixture

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:53.240
of what's coming
in from the window

00:27:53.240 --> 00:27:55.973
and what's coming in
from the key light

00:27:55.973 --> 00:27:57.890
because we don't want
the background-- we want

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:00.265
the background to be a little
bit darker than the subject

00:28:00.265 --> 00:28:04.250
so the subject pops out
as the main element.

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:06.110
Because one of the
things that we're

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:08.480
thinking about when
we're lighting is we're

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:09.560
drawing attention.

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:12.680
We're using lighting to draw
the viewer's attention to what

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:14.270
the important things
are in the frame.

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:16.340
But we don't want to
completely obscure

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:19.670
the background, because while
the interview subject may

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:23.270
be the subject of our image,
there's also a predicate, just

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:24.570
like any good sentence.

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:28.160
And so the context here
is the editing room,

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.990
is the equipment
that's in the room.

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:31.790
So we want to feature that, too.

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:35.120
But we want it to feel like
it's part of the background,

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:39.110
both in terms of intensity
and in terms of placement.

00:28:39.110 --> 00:28:42.140
So a little bit of
background light

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:45.080
is being provided by
both spill from the key

00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:46.490
as well as some of the windows.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:49.970
So what we did is we
varied that foam core

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:52.530
until we had just the right
amount of ambient light

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:53.030
in the room.

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:54.380
And that's where we placed it.

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:58.190
So very often, you can
use nature, natural light,

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:00.500
as one of your light units.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:02.720
I like to work with
what's natural.

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:04.940
I knew that was
facing in a direction

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:08.090
that we wouldn't have direct
sun shining in the room

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:09.470
any time during the interview.

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:13.010
So it's always good to know what
direction the sun's going in.

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.830
So we could rely for
two hours that we

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:18.830
would have skylight
but no direct sunlight

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:21.000
to mess up our scene.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:24.380
So if I can bring Mother Nature
into the lighting equation,

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:25.310
that's great.

00:29:25.310 --> 00:29:28.460
If I could do this whole setup
without any artificial light,

00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:30.570
that would be even better.

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:34.140
But that doesn't always
work out that way.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:37.960
So I'm going to basically
review what we just talked about

00:29:37.960 --> 00:29:40.700
in four different environments.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:43.840
So a typical formal interview
is what you just saw.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:47.435
In this particular
situation, I saw

00:29:47.435 --> 00:29:49.810
that there were these beautiful
French doors in the room.

00:29:49.810 --> 00:29:53.410
I said, aha, we're going to
use that as our background

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.480
because it looks nice.

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:57.520
It adds a little
bit of dimension.

00:29:57.520 --> 00:29:59.040
But I want the
background to recede,

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:00.790
and I want the foreground
to move forward.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:03.820
Well, it turns out that your
warmer colors have a tendency

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:06.640
to move forward, and your
cooler colors, like your blues,

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:08.770
have a tendency to fall
into the background.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:09.820
So what did I do?

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:12.880
I put a background light
behind the lace curtains,

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:16.150
behind those French doors,
with a half blue gel.

00:30:16.150 --> 00:30:20.080
So that's giving me
the bluish background.

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:22.690
And of course, she's
being lit by a soft box,

00:30:22.690 --> 00:30:25.840
something called a
Rifa-Lite from Lowel.

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:31.990
So the primary key in this case
is a relatively medium sized,

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:33.170
I guess, soft light.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:35.800
And once again, we're using
the reflector Flexfill

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:39.190
as our full light because the
less wires and the less light

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:40.850
that I can travel
with, the better.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:45.310
So we basically lit this
situation with two lights

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.500
plus the ambient
spill that was coming

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.490
from the windows in
the middle of the day.

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:52.960
So this was, once again, a
three point lighting setup,

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:54.820
but we're only
using two fixtures

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:56.950
because we're making use
of the natural light that

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:01.420
was available in this
living room at the time.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:03.040
And the key is relatively soft.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:04.900
And you can tell that
by the way the light

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:07.100
wraps around the face.

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.850
So notice how even
though that there's

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:12.610
a shadow where her
nose is and there's

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:14.560
a shadow on the right
hand side of her face,

00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:18.340
there is a smooth gradient from
the light to the dark area.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:24.070
And we call that the way the
light wraps around the face.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:26.650
We'll see now in
this other interview,

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:30.010
we have a harsh,
overhead, standard Edison

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:34.090
based light bulb, 150
watt screw-in light bulb

00:31:34.090 --> 00:31:35.750
in the top of the room.

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:38.440
There's also a couple
of small quartz lights

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:43.450
that are lighting the toy
collection cabinet behind her.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:47.860
But this is basically
working under very harsh,

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:52.150
non-optimal lighting conditions.

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:59.080
And so it's not at all
flattering in some ways.

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:01.270
We're not doing our
subject justice.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:05.110
Of course, in this
film, she was playfully

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:07.280
playing the role of the bad guy.

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:10.030
So the lighting
worked dramatically

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:12.260
for those purposes.

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:15.250
So I decided to go
with it rather than

00:32:15.250 --> 00:32:18.670
bringing the lighting kit out
because we said it looks OK.

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.670
We want this to
be raw and harsh.

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:23.470
Once again, it's
all about context.

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:27.430
What does the lighting
do for your storytelling?

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:32.350
It was OK for a character
making certain harsh comments

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:36.310
to be in slightly harsh light.

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:39.220
At least it worked
for me creatively

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:40.600
in this particular scene.

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:45.010
But most of the time,
that's not what I want.

00:32:45.010 --> 00:32:48.490
Here's another situation
which is not at all optimal.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:54.520
This is handheld,
impromptu conversation

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:58.060
with a character in the
middle of an art opening.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:00.400
The lighting was
mixed, window light,

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.740
dark corners,
incandescent lights,

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:05.740
harsh, really, really difficult.
I looked around the room,

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:08.450
and I go, oh my God,
what are we going to do?

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:10.090
And then I saw this big window.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:13.670
And I said, do you mind
standing next to the big window?

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:16.930
So out of the darkness, I
was able to move the subject

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:23.410
so that she was being
lit by window light.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:27.970
So here's me, no lights, no
kit, camera in my right hand,

00:33:27.970 --> 00:33:30.980
microphone in the left hand.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:32.680
Let's move by the window.

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:34.810
So sometimes, you just
got to look around

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:36.910
and see where the
light looks best

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:41.165
and move your subject
into that area.

00:33:41.165 --> 00:33:42.790
PROFESSOR: And it's
remembering, again,

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:45.760
to apply some of the principles
that you're talking about

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.860
in a situation where
you don't have lights

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:50.840
but still keeping in
mind all of the things

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:53.470
that we're discussing, the
intensity of the light,

00:33:53.470 --> 00:34:00.922
the direction of the light,
the quality of the light,

00:34:00.922 --> 00:34:04.750
the color of the light,
and making choices on where

00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:07.835
you conduct your interviews
or do your shooting--

00:34:07.835 --> 00:34:08.710
DAVID TAMES: Exactly.

00:34:08.710 --> 00:34:09.880
PROFESSOR: --based
on those principles.

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:11.409
DAVID TAMES: In
fact, in this case,

00:34:11.409 --> 00:34:14.230
I didn't even have time
to do my 8th blue trick.

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:16.659
I just set the
preset to daylight

00:34:16.659 --> 00:34:21.909
because I knew that the window
was pretty much daylight.

00:34:21.909 --> 00:34:23.770
And of course, you're
getting the little bit

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:27.520
of triangle on the cheek
because of her position

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:28.630
relative to the light.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:33.380
So that is what I'm always
paying attention to.

00:34:33.380 --> 00:34:35.679
I watch somebody
talking, and I go,

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:38.600
am I seeing that
dimension light?

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.270
And in fact, when I'm doing an
impromptu interview like this,

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:44.330
I won't ask the
subject to change.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:47.540
Very often, I will
move and watch

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:49.340
how the lighting
changes on their face.

00:34:49.340 --> 00:34:52.014
Because sometimes just moving
to the left a foot and a half

00:34:52.014 --> 00:34:53.389
or to the right
a foot and a half

00:34:53.389 --> 00:34:54.847
will completely
change the lighting

00:34:54.847 --> 00:34:57.350
when somebody moves their
face like this to look at you

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:58.658
or like this to look at you.

00:34:58.658 --> 00:35:00.200
So those are the
things that you want

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:03.350
to get in the habit of
always thinking about.

00:35:03.350 --> 00:35:05.860
It becomes this automatic
background process,

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:12.690
sort of like background
rendering or whatever.

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:17.422
And of course, I do like
dramatic chiaroscuro sometimes.

00:35:17.422 --> 00:35:19.880
This was a particular moment
where the character is talking

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:21.950
about how he first
came to Boston

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:25.100
and he found it to be a
cool and uninviting place

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:27.500
and people weren't
very friendly.

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:30.110
And so even though most of
the interviews in this film

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:33.270
are shot in natural
light, I decided

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:37.658
to shoot this one
dramatic and dark.

00:35:37.658 --> 00:35:39.770
This is where the
character starts

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:42.410
to realize certain things.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:45.200
And it's a very personal,
intimate moment.

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:51.380
So we said, OK, let's do
the more chiaroscuro thing.

00:35:51.380 --> 00:35:56.060
So you have the light
coming from the side.

00:35:56.060 --> 00:35:59.150
And we're using actually
pretty relatively intense

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:02.760
blue back lights
for the background

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.760
so that the background
doesn't go totally black.

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:06.693
But it's relatively dark.

00:36:06.693 --> 00:36:08.360
So this is a little
more along the lines

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:10.580
of Caravaggio versus Vermeer.

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:13.517
PROFESSOR: And you can also
see the theatricality that you

00:36:13.517 --> 00:36:14.600
were talking about before.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:14.870
DAVID TAMES: Yeah.

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.710
PROFESSOR: I mean, it might work
for this subject in the case.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:16.722
DAVID TAMES: Right.

00:36:16.722 --> 00:36:17.570
It worked in this case.

00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:17.900
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:19.370
DAVID TAMES: And it's
really the only interview

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:21.350
in the entire film
that's lit this way.

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:24.290
This was kind of a
brief, theatrical moment

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:25.340
in some respects.

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:26.990
But yeah, it has
that theatricality.

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:29.720
It's not at all natural.

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:32.240
This is of somebody feeling
like a fish out of water.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:34.880
I wanted to create an image
that didn't feel like it

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:37.160
fit with the rest of the film.

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:45.350
So these are four
different approaches

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:47.040
to an interview situation.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:50.220
And if you take a
look at the PDF,

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:54.440
I've synthesized what I have
found from seven or eight

00:36:54.440 --> 00:37:03.800
different sources,
basically, as you

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:09.140
change the position of the key
light relative to the subject,

00:37:09.140 --> 00:37:13.160
you go from this continuum from
glamor lighting on the left,

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:15.470
where the key light
and the fill light

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:17.720
are actually directly
overhead, there's

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:20.850
a small little butterfly
shadow under the nose

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:22.610
and there's a fill
light filling that

00:37:22.610 --> 00:37:25.470
in so it's not a harsh
shadow under the nose

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:27.080
and this kind of
Hollywood lighting

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:29.585
where the key is almost
in front of the subject.

00:37:29.585 --> 00:37:32.450
It gives you this very
glamorous look, especially

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:35.120
if you have high cheekbones.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:38.780
But it's not at all natural,
but it is very glamorous.

00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:41.540
And then as you move the
fill light to the left,

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:44.030
in the middle here, you
get to the Rembrandt style,

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:45.470
which is my tendency.

00:37:45.470 --> 00:37:47.750
I like the light
coming from the left.

00:37:47.750 --> 00:37:51.920
I also find in situations where
I'm using a small hand-held LED

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:54.560
light to give a little bit
of supplemental lighting

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:57.428
in an otherwise verite
situation, if I put

00:37:57.428 --> 00:37:58.970
the light to the
left of the subject,

00:37:58.970 --> 00:38:01.070
not only do they look
better and more dimensional,

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:03.800
but it's not glaring
in their eye.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:07.110
And that's actually
more comfortable.

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:10.220
And then, of course,
profile lighting,

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:14.510
basically the key is
behind the subject

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:17.120
and you're emphasizing
their profile.

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:18.710
I mean, that's really dramatic.

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:22.370
You would do that
for an interview

00:38:22.370 --> 00:38:25.430
with somebody whose identity
you want to obscure.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:28.875
You kill the fill light,
and you have a backlight

00:38:28.875 --> 00:38:29.750
coming from the back.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:34.205
And you just see this
outline rim of a person.

00:38:34.205 --> 00:38:38.300
What this slide is about
is to encourage you--

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:40.880
when I've taught a detailed
cinematography class,

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:43.280
I actually have the
students set of these

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.830
up and shoot all
these different setups

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:50.720
and reflect on how the
lighting changes as you

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:53.014
move the key light around.

00:38:53.014 --> 00:38:56.862
It'd be cool to have a 3D
simulator environment to try

00:38:56.862 --> 00:38:57.570
these things out.

00:38:57.570 --> 00:38:59.820
But there's nothing like
going out into the real world

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:03.630
with your lighting kit and
trying these different setups.

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:06.140
So we've talked
about soft light.

00:39:06.140 --> 00:39:07.790
We've talked about hard lights.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:12.110
Some common lights that you find
available in the market today,

00:39:12.110 --> 00:39:13.490
there's a variety
of soft lights.

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:15.720
They're very popular,
since if you're

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:17.720
going to do a simple,
quick, and dirty interview

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:19.262
with a single light,
typically you'll

00:39:19.262 --> 00:39:22.010
just pull out a soft light.

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:23.630
A popular soft
light is something

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:24.680
called the Lowel Rifa.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:26.660
It kind of pops open
like an umbrella.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:28.250
You throw a diffuser
on the front,

00:39:28.250 --> 00:39:31.100
you hook it up to the stand,
and it sets up very quickly,

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:35.270
and instant soft light.

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:37.100
Although they're a
little more expensive,

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:40.310
I love the Kino Flo Diva Lite.

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:44.600
This is a fluorescent light
fixture that has four,

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.390
or two depending on the
size, fluorescent bulbs.

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:49.400
And you can pop
in either daylight

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:52.160
balanced fluorescents or
tungsten balanced fluorescents.

00:39:52.160 --> 00:39:54.860
I've done a lot of interviews
outdoors, under a tree,

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:57.680
in the shade, and
brought in the Diva

00:39:57.680 --> 00:39:59.630
with the daylight
balanced light just

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:01.460
for a little bit of
sparkle in the eyes,

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:03.590
just for a little bit
of extra definition.

00:40:03.590 --> 00:40:07.070
And all of a sudden, it takes
something in the shade and soft

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:09.740
and gives it just a little
bit of dimensionality.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:11.930
And you can't do that
unless your light source

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:13.040
is daylight balanced.

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:15.980
And the Diva is just
barely bright enough

00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:17.390
to use in the shade.

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:19.410
No light source is
going to work in the sun

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.280
unless you have a
Hollywood grip truck.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:25.520
But for indoor interviews,
a couple of Divas

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:28.790
is just spectacular
in terms of soft light

00:40:28.790 --> 00:40:31.760
with just a little
bit of directionality.

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:33.710
And the fact that you
can pop in and out

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:38.090
the fluorescent light tubes
for either daylight or tungsten

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:39.240
is really awesome.

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:42.980
It comes in a little suitcase
with both sets of bulbs.

00:40:42.980 --> 00:40:44.510
And off you go.

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:47.130
Open face lights are
much less expensive.

00:40:47.130 --> 00:40:49.790
They're relatively
cheap, anything

00:40:49.790 --> 00:40:55.430
from a $30 work light at Home
Depot to a Lowel Omni-Light.

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:57.470
They come in all sorts
of shapes and sizes.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:02.030
Typically, they have
a parabolic reflector.

00:41:02.030 --> 00:41:04.370
And then we'll talk in
a moment about Fresnels.

00:41:04.370 --> 00:41:05.660
Fresnels are a little fancier.

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:08.570
They actually have
a lens system.

00:41:08.570 --> 00:41:11.210
PARs-- and of course, what
you see here in the picture

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:13.460
is a PAR with a soft
box on top of it.

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:15.660
PARs are very
efficient light sources

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:17.660
because they have this
parabolic reflector built

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.840
in to the light unit.

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:24.200
And they're often available in
this high technology, daylight

00:41:24.200 --> 00:41:27.080
balanced light called an HMI.

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:30.500
Small PARS in the
300 watt range are

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:33.140
very popular with
documentary crews

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:35.913
because you can power
them off a small battery.

00:41:35.913 --> 00:41:37.580
Their daylight balance,
you can use them

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:41.300
outdoors to get rid of
shadows and stuff like that.

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:43.040
And then if you
need to soften them,

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.470
you can always pop a
soft box in the front.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:48.170
Or if you need
that hard intensity

00:41:48.170 --> 00:41:52.820
efficiency of the PAR unit, you
can just pull the soft box off.

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:55.400
So they're very versatile
in that respect.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:59.780
And in fact, here in Boston,
the local rental house rents

00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:01.310
something called a Kobold.

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:02.620
Did I say that right?

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:03.950
Kobald?

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:07.580
Small, HMI PAR, which
is really useful.

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:13.970
But if you're on a tight
budget, you don't necessarily

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:16.010
have to go out and spend
thousands of dollars

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:17.210
on professional lighting.

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:20.820
Mother Nature usually provides
her services for free.

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:23.990
You can use practical
household bulbs.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:28.850
Popular among some folks are
simple photo flood lamps,

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:31.670
which are very bright
incandescent lamps that you can

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:33.290
put in inexpensive fixtures.

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:35.510
It turns out, fluorescent
utility lights,

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:37.700
ordinary fluorescent
lights, you can actually

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:41.090
go to your local
hardware supply store

00:42:41.090 --> 00:42:47.120
and buy daylight balanced or
tungsten balanced fluorescent

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:52.070
tubes, Chroma 50s and Ultra
32s I think they're called.

00:42:52.070 --> 00:42:54.800
And you can put them in
ordinary fluorescent fixtures.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:57.950
Chinese lanterns are awesome,
these paper little lanterns.

00:42:57.950 --> 00:43:00.920
But I wouldn't use more
than a 60 or, pushing it,

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:03.710
100 watt bulb in these
paper lanterns and never

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:05.300
leave them unattended.

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:07.070
Regardless of how
flame retardant

00:43:07.070 --> 00:43:10.680
the manufacturers say they are,
I wouldn't leave them alone.

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:13.070
But they give you this
beautiful soft light.

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:14.840
Now, there's a
professional version

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:17.720
of these lanterns
called Chimera lanterns.

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:22.070
And they're made of a special
high heat resistant material.

00:43:22.070 --> 00:43:24.650
And you can put a pretty
powerful 1,000 watt lamp

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:27.770
inside one of those and get
this beautiful, beautiful soft

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:29.720
light.

00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:32.810
But I've seen a lot of people
get a lot of mileage out

00:43:32.810 --> 00:43:36.140
of ordinary work lights.

00:43:36.140 --> 00:43:39.650
I know a lot of animation folks
use ordinary hardware store

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:41.240
work lights for their
lighting source.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:43.850
You can clip a little diffusion
in the front if you need to.

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:44.660
They're cheap.

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:46.520
The only downside
is that they don't

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:49.500
fold up nicely for travel.

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:52.220
But if you're doing
animation work at home,

00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:55.340
those inexpensive work
lights work great.

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:58.610
And you can actually buy
tungsten balanced 3,200 degree

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:00.350
Kelvin bulbs for them.

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:03.050
So on camera lights,
some people like them.

00:44:03.050 --> 00:44:06.290
I personally don't
like on camera lights

00:44:06.290 --> 00:44:09.990
because I really don't like
the flat look that they create.

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:13.190
I prefer to hold a camera off
to the side, find an assistant,

00:44:13.190 --> 00:44:17.030
find somebody to hold the
light off to the side.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:18.980
I've even done it myself.

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:21.200
Your arm gets really,
really, really tired.

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:24.230
But it pays off by
not having that deer

00:44:24.230 --> 00:44:27.230
caught in the headlights
look of the light

00:44:27.230 --> 00:44:28.320
directly on the camera.

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:30.800
People don't like being blinded.

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:33.470
Get the light off to the side
is the moral of the story.

00:44:33.470 --> 00:44:35.630
But these small battery
powered lights, especially

00:44:35.630 --> 00:44:38.420
the new generation of
light emitting diode LED,

00:44:38.420 --> 00:44:42.080
lights are spectacular because
I've been able to shoot all day

00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:45.500
with four camera batteries
and a couple of LED lights,

00:44:45.500 --> 00:44:49.470
not on continuously,
but it can work.

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:50.810
I have a thing for Fresnels.

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:54.200
I really like the
quality of their light.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:56.870
They were originally developed
by Augustine Fresnel,

00:44:56.870 --> 00:45:00.500
who contributed to the
theory of color and optics.

00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:02.870
And the French
Lighthouse Commission

00:45:02.870 --> 00:45:06.770
had a problem in
the early 1800s.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:09.470
Those whale oil lamps
weren't very bright.

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:11.960
Ships were going into rocks.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:16.940
A lot of sailors and merchandise
was being lost at sea.

00:45:16.940 --> 00:45:20.820
And they commissioned Fresnel
to solve the lighthouse problem.

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:23.180
Well, he realized,
you know what?

00:45:23.180 --> 00:45:27.590
If we put a whole bunch of
these triangular pieces of glass

00:45:27.590 --> 00:45:30.020
in the right
arrangement, we can take

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:32.600
all of those light rays
that are going astray

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:35.780
and send them all off,
single line, out to sea.

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:38.900
And all of a sudden, these
teeny tiny little whale

00:45:38.900 --> 00:45:43.160
oil powered lamps
were super bright

00:45:43.160 --> 00:45:46.340
because all of the light rays
are going single file where

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:47.810
they're supposed
to be out to sea

00:45:47.810 --> 00:45:50.720
instead of lighting the
side of the lighthouse.

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:52.220
Who cares about that, right?

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:53.780
So what's interesting
about Fresnels

00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:56.960
is that they're
very crisp, clean,

00:45:56.960 --> 00:46:00.230
focus lights that cast
perfectly clean shadows.

00:46:00.230 --> 00:46:04.010
They have the same quality
of light as direct sunlight.

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:05.510
They're great for backlights.

00:46:05.510 --> 00:46:08.177
They're great for when you
want that quality of light

00:46:08.177 --> 00:46:09.260
coming through the window.

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:11.060
They're a little heavier
and more expensive

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:14.000
because they have this
scalloped glass lens, which

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:17.810
you might recognize the pattern
from lighthouses, which we have

00:46:17.810 --> 00:46:20.180
a lot of here in New England.

00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:22.670
And so the units are
a little heavier.

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:26.270
But they have a little knob
where you can focus them.

00:46:26.270 --> 00:46:29.910
And they have that nice,
crisp quality of light.

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:31.850
So I have a thing for Fresnels.

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:32.900
I really like them.

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:36.500
I own five myself.

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:38.480
But they are heavy
to carry around.

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:39.560
That's the price you pay.

00:46:39.560 --> 00:46:43.700
They are physically bigger
and physically heavier.

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:47.000
Here's a chart comparing
different lighting

00:46:47.000 --> 00:46:47.690
technologies.

00:46:47.690 --> 00:46:50.120
Because not only do we have
different configuration

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:55.370
of lights, like open
face, soft light, soft--

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:57.710
open face Fresnels,
soft light, et cetera,

00:46:57.710 --> 00:46:59.630
in terms of the
physical configuration

00:46:59.630 --> 00:47:03.680
of the light source versus the
lens unit and things like that,

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:07.040
but there's basically
four technologies

00:47:07.040 --> 00:47:09.560
that we use today in
professional lighting

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:12.140
for providing the
source of light.

00:47:12.140 --> 00:47:16.730
Typical tungsten incandescent
lamps are the least expensive.

00:47:16.730 --> 00:47:18.290
But they use up a lot of energy.

00:47:18.290 --> 00:47:20.540
Unfortunately,
incandescent lights,

00:47:20.540 --> 00:47:22.400
they are actually
space heaters that

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:24.860
happen to give a small
percentage of their energy

00:47:24.860 --> 00:47:26.540
out in the light range.

00:47:26.540 --> 00:47:31.700
But maybe 30% is light and the
rest of the energy is heat.

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:32.660
They're very hot.

00:47:32.660 --> 00:47:35.360
And on a hot summer
day, they're just

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:37.370
really uncomfortable
to be close to.

00:47:37.370 --> 00:47:40.370
HMI is newer
technology, daylight

00:47:40.370 --> 00:47:43.683
balanced, very high tech units.

00:47:43.683 --> 00:47:44.600
But they're expensive.

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:46.370
They require a lot
of electronics.

00:47:46.370 --> 00:47:51.140
They require very sophisticated
lamps with rare Earth gases

00:47:51.140 --> 00:47:53.723
and all kinds of fancy stuff.

00:47:53.723 --> 00:47:54.890
But their daylight balanced.

00:47:54.890 --> 00:47:58.760
And they're very bright
and very efficient.

00:47:58.760 --> 00:48:02.210
Halfway in between
are fluorescents.

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:05.150
They make great soft lights.

00:48:05.150 --> 00:48:07.162
They run very cool.

00:48:07.162 --> 00:48:09.620
They're not necessarily the
best thing for the environment,

00:48:09.620 --> 00:48:12.590
but neither are the
other lighting sources.

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:15.140
But they run cool, and
they use less power.

00:48:15.140 --> 00:48:19.230
And the Kino Flo fluorescent
lights are just awesome.

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:22.160
And up and coming,
they are LEDs.

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:25.260
LEDs have come a long way
in the last few years.

00:48:25.260 --> 00:48:28.430
They're available in these
panels of either daylight

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:30.890
balanced or tungsten balanced.

00:48:30.890 --> 00:48:33.150
In fact, light panels
has a really nice grid

00:48:33.150 --> 00:48:34.520
that's a bicolor panel.

00:48:34.520 --> 00:48:39.050
It's a grid of both daylight
and tungsten balanced LEDs.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:42.260
And you can dial in how
much you want of each.

00:48:42.260 --> 00:48:45.470
There's also a small
version called the Blender,

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:48.590
which actually has
a grid of daylight

00:48:48.590 --> 00:48:50.210
and tungsten balanced LEDs.

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:52.980
And you can dial in the
color temperature you want.

00:48:52.980 --> 00:48:55.400
So they're very
expensive compared

00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:57.050
to other light sources.

00:48:57.050 --> 00:48:58.640
But they're very versatile.

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:01.460
The zeolite is a
model of LED light

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:03.290
that you can dial in
any color because it

00:49:03.290 --> 00:49:06.260
uses a red, green,
blue grid of LEDs.

00:49:06.260 --> 00:49:09.830
There's some really nice, new
products in the LED department.

00:49:09.830 --> 00:49:12.500
And while they're
expensive, their lightness

00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:15.020
is just awesome.

00:49:15.020 --> 00:49:19.220
If you go all LEDs, you
can cut the weight and size

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:21.630
of your lighting kit in half.

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:25.640
So there's something to
be said for high tech.

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:28.340
If you're just starting out, you
don't have any lighting at all

00:49:28.340 --> 00:49:31.640
and you'd like a little
something to start with,

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:37.460
one suggestion is an open
face light with an umbrella.

00:49:37.460 --> 00:49:40.010
Lowel makes this light
called the Tota-Light.

00:49:40.010 --> 00:49:42.890
And they also have a newer
version called the V-Light,

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:45.200
which is a little safer.

00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:49.760
But this is basically just
an open face light unit.

00:49:49.760 --> 00:49:53.840
And used with an umbrella
bouncing the light,

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:57.140
you can now have a choice
between a hard light

00:49:57.140 --> 00:49:58.440
or a soft light.

00:49:58.440 --> 00:50:02.160
And so you can get a very
quick and easy lightweight,

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:06.500
soft source by bouncing
the Tota off the umbrella.

00:50:06.500 --> 00:50:07.790
And there's your soft light.

00:50:07.790 --> 00:50:09.690
And you can put
this kit together,

00:50:09.690 --> 00:50:13.400
including a small light
stand, for less than $250

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:16.670
from your favorite
online equipment retailer

00:50:16.670 --> 00:50:21.020
or your local photo shop
that sells video and lighting

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:22.430
equipment.

00:50:22.430 --> 00:50:25.820
So this is the Lowel
Tota-Light or the Lowel V-Light

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:27.710
as a great starter kit.

00:50:27.710 --> 00:50:30.110
You can put this in
a teeny duffel bag.

00:50:30.110 --> 00:50:33.260
I've often taken something like
this with me on the train down

00:50:33.260 --> 00:50:36.260
to New York, camera on one
shoulder with sound gear

00:50:36.260 --> 00:50:38.480
and this on the other shoulder.

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:42.470
Very light, simple, effective.

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:46.980
Then over time, you might
want to add additional units

00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:49.580
depending on your
particular lighting style.

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:53.370
A lot of people ask me, what
lighting kit do you recommend?

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:56.540
Well, it's really hard for me
to recommend a lighting kit

00:50:56.540 --> 00:51:00.590
because it really depends
on what your preferences are

00:51:00.590 --> 00:51:02.610
and what you want to accomplish.

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:06.300
So I would suggest buy
one light at a time,

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:09.740
learn it, get to know it, and
figure out what you want next.

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:12.680
Start with a basic soft
light and go from there.

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:14.180
You might want to
add some Fresnels.

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:16.070
You might want to
add some LED lights.

00:51:16.070 --> 00:51:18.650
You might want to add a
Flexfill reflector, which you'll

00:51:18.650 --> 00:51:21.080
see in the live demo portion.

00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:24.470
So these are various things
that you might want to add.

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:25.670
But explore.

00:51:25.670 --> 00:51:28.010
Use it, and figure
out what you need.

00:51:28.010 --> 00:51:30.560
A lot of towns
have rental houses

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:34.250
that you can rent light kits,
rent a kit for a weekend,

00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:36.113
get to know it,
decide what you like,

00:51:36.113 --> 00:51:38.030
what works for you, what
doesn't work for you.

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:42.870
Just remember, it all starts
with a good key light.

00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:45.620
So definitely at
some point, you want

00:51:45.620 --> 00:51:48.350
to have something
like a Lowel Rifa-Lite

00:51:48.350 --> 00:51:56.540
or a Kino Flo Diva Light
as your staple soft light.

00:51:56.540 --> 00:51:59.300
And then we talked
about diffusion material

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:00.210
that you can get.

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:04.487
It sort of looks like opaque
gel that will soften any light.

00:52:04.487 --> 00:52:07.070
That's why it's nice to have a
few hard lights because you can

00:52:07.070 --> 00:52:09.450
always soften them with gels.

00:52:09.450 --> 00:52:11.760
Color changing gels,
there's this whole series

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:15.990
called CTB for Color Temperature
Blue, CTO for Color Temperature

00:52:15.990 --> 00:52:16.740
Orange.

00:52:16.740 --> 00:52:18.720
You can use these
gels for changing

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:21.120
the color of light sources.

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:22.890
Black wrap is an
interesting material.

00:52:22.890 --> 00:52:25.950
It's aluminum foil with
flame retardant black paint.

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.195
You can use that--

00:52:27.195 --> 00:52:29.400
if light is spilling
from a light unit,

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:32.940
you use the black wrap to
kill the spill off the light.

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:36.120
And you won't start any fires
because it's flame retardant

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:37.330
and it's metal foil.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:38.760
Does get hot though.

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:43.860
And several manufacturers make
these great little wrapping

00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:47.750
things called jelly rolls that
you can roll up your gels in.

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:51.270
And they come in both
small and large sizes.

00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:53.940
Dimmers are handy
for dimming lights.

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:58.860
But I'll warn you now that they
create 60 cycle hum and buzz.

00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:03.330
And very often, the filament
of lights will buzz.

00:53:03.330 --> 00:53:06.157
So if you carry
dimmers on the set,

00:53:06.157 --> 00:53:07.740
if you're working
with a sound person,

00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:10.840
they will talk to you about it.

00:53:10.840 --> 00:53:13.440
So dimmers are
useful, but they're

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:17.520
tricky because of the
interference problems.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:21.630
But in some cases,
they can be used.

00:53:21.630 --> 00:53:26.130
Cube taps are useful for
turning one outlet into three.

00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:27.980
You want to be careful
with those two prong

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:28.980
to three prong adapters.

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:30.900
But in a lot of
older houses, they

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:35.370
don't have three prong
plugs, my own included.

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:37.650
Gloves are handy if you--

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:40.143
I usually handle
lights without gloves

00:53:40.143 --> 00:53:42.060
because I know exactly
where the hot parts are

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:44.850
and where the hot parts aren't
from years of experience.

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:46.530
But when you're
starting out, you

00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:49.980
might want to use
gloves and be careful.

00:53:49.980 --> 00:53:52.860
Gaffer tape will
solve any problem

00:53:52.860 --> 00:53:55.710
that the lighting instruments
can't solve in most cases.

00:53:55.710 --> 00:54:00.450
And I won't go into detail,
but as you expand your lighting

00:54:00.450 --> 00:54:03.480
work, you might want to add
things like flags and nets

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:04.830
for sculpting light.

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:07.150
But that's beyond the
scope of this talk.

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:09.000
If you ever have
larger lights, you're

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:11.370
going to need to put
sandbags on the stands.

00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:15.250
So having some portable
beach is very, very handy.

00:54:15.250 --> 00:54:17.460
And there's the
universal C-stand

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:20.670
that can be used for
all sorts of things,

00:54:20.670 --> 00:54:22.320
hanging sound blankets
in addition to

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:24.570
holding lights and stuff.

00:54:24.570 --> 00:54:26.930
And there's a lot of
gripology that we're not

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:27.930
going to get into today.

00:54:27.930 --> 00:54:31.260
But it's good to know that
there is this whole collection

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:34.710
of gear out there.

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:36.900
But some that are
useful is if you're

00:54:36.900 --> 00:54:40.020
working in an environment
with drop ceilings,

00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:41.880
these little scissor
clamps are great,

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:43.950
which will give you a
standard 5/8 inch stud

00:54:43.950 --> 00:54:45.300
for hanging a small light.

00:54:45.300 --> 00:54:47.550
It's good to have a couple
of those in your kit.

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:50.400
Mafer clamps are very handy,
also known as photo clamps

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:52.332
depending on who you ask.

00:54:52.332 --> 00:54:53.790
These are great
clamps that you can

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:58.590
attach to pipes and all sorts of
conduit and things if you need.

00:54:58.590 --> 00:55:00.720
You'll notice that
for all small lights,

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:03.840
the industry standard
is a 5/8 inch stud.

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:06.030
It's also known as a baby stud.

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:07.990
Grip clips are great.

00:55:07.990 --> 00:55:09.480
You can buy official grip clips.

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:11.772
You can go to the hardware
store and buy the equivalent

00:55:11.772 --> 00:55:13.830
for a fraction of the price.

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:15.735
I still use a light
meter sometimes

00:55:15.735 --> 00:55:16.860
when I'm setting up lights.

00:55:16.860 --> 00:55:20.100
But it's probably
an anachronism.

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.530
Though it is handy to
have an 18% grade card,

00:55:22.530 --> 00:55:24.480
white on one side
for white balance,

00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:26.130
gray on the other side.

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:28.320
Very often, if you're
color correcting a scene

00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:30.930
and you put that gray
card in the scene,

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:32.980
and now you have
a gray reference.

00:55:32.980 --> 00:55:35.890
So if you're color
obsessed like myself,

00:55:35.890 --> 00:55:39.210
you might want to carry
a gray card in your kit.

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:41.777
And there's a list
here of other things.

00:55:41.777 --> 00:55:43.360
This is sort of a
homework assignment.

00:55:43.360 --> 00:55:45.000
Figure out what all
these things are.

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:48.210
It's a good thing to learn.

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:50.970
At the end of the
day, just remember,

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:55.740
there are no rules about what is
good lighting and bad lighting

00:55:55.740 --> 00:55:58.410
because one person's bad
lighting is another person's

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:00.690
dramatic lighting.

00:56:00.690 --> 00:56:03.810
It might be bad if it
creates the wrong ambiance

00:56:03.810 --> 00:56:08.580
for the scene or produces an
unintended unflattering effect

00:56:08.580 --> 00:56:10.223
or it distracts the
viewer's attention.

00:56:10.223 --> 00:56:11.640
Remember, we were
talking about we

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:17.490
use light to drive the viewer's
attention in the scene.

00:56:17.490 --> 00:56:20.700
But if it's not doing that,
then maybe it is bad lighting.

00:56:20.700 --> 00:56:24.150
Does it prevent the viewer
from seeing what's important?

00:56:24.150 --> 00:56:26.110
Or is it showing things
that are irrelevant?

00:56:26.110 --> 00:56:29.190
So one of the biggest problems
with point and shoot video

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:32.100
is that oftentimes our
subject is obscured,

00:56:32.100 --> 00:56:34.080
and something that's
completely unimportant

00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:37.270
is staring us right at the face.

00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:40.350
So think about, what
is it doing for you

00:56:40.350 --> 00:56:42.420
and your own storytelling?

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:45.210
Good lighting allows us
to do many, many things.

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:47.160
I'm not going to go
through this whole list.

00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:50.280
But once again, it can
help us draw attention

00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:51.900
to what's important
in the scene.

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:53.910
It can help isolate the subject.

00:56:53.910 --> 00:56:55.320
It could imply a season.

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:57.150
It could establish a locale.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:57:00.210
It can concentrate attention to
a particular part of the scene.

00:57:00.210 --> 00:57:03.870
It can emphasize form, like we
talked about with chiaroscuro.

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:07.620
Or we can de-emphasize
form with flat lighting.

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:10.420
At the end of the
day, it about altering

00:57:10.420 --> 00:57:14.890
the viewer's emotional response
and establishing a mood.

00:57:14.890 --> 00:57:18.370
And so here's kind of my
laundry list of all the things

00:57:18.370 --> 00:57:20.590
that lighting can do for you.

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:22.970
And through experience
and through practice,

00:57:22.970 --> 00:57:25.690
you will see how what
you do with lighting

00:57:25.690 --> 00:57:29.620
has an impact in
your storytelling.

00:57:29.620 --> 00:57:31.600
So that's a list
of all the things

00:57:31.600 --> 00:57:34.490
that lighting can do for you.

00:57:34.490 --> 00:57:38.860
And at the end here,
there's a brief glossary

00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:42.430
of terms of
enlightenment, which is

00:57:42.430 --> 00:57:44.530
a word I borrowed
from Ross Lowell, who

00:57:44.530 --> 00:57:47.890
wrote a wonderful book called
Matters of Light and Depth,

00:57:47.890 --> 00:57:51.350
which I highly recommend.

00:57:51.350 --> 00:57:54.760
There's electricity
involved in lighting.

00:57:54.760 --> 00:57:59.920
And you want to be very careful
and treat it with respect.

00:57:59.920 --> 00:58:03.100
And if you have frayed
wires or anything damaged

00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:04.750
in your lighting
equipment, you want

00:58:04.750 --> 00:58:08.290
to fix it before you
plug it into the wall.

00:58:08.290 --> 00:58:12.620
What you have here is a simple
calculator for determining,

00:58:12.620 --> 00:58:17.230
do you have enough power
to provide the power

00:58:17.230 --> 00:58:18.400
for your lighting units?

00:58:18.400 --> 00:58:23.950
So the way to remember it is
a 20 amp circuit, a 20 amp

00:58:23.950 --> 00:58:26.890
household circuit, which is
pretty standard in bathrooms

00:58:26.890 --> 00:58:29.530
and kitchens in
contemporary construction--

00:58:29.530 --> 00:58:32.800
in a lot of older houses, you
will find 15 amp circuits--

00:58:32.800 --> 00:58:35.110
but the thing to
remember, a 20 amp circuit

00:58:35.110 --> 00:58:37.600
can support 2,000 watts.

00:58:37.600 --> 00:58:39.490
That's the only number
you need to remember.

00:58:39.490 --> 00:58:41.620
And you can
extrapolate from there.

00:58:41.620 --> 00:58:47.170
A 20 amp circuit can
feed to 2,000 watts

00:58:47.170 --> 00:58:54.040
because you just basically take
the amps and multiply by 100.

00:58:54.040 --> 00:58:59.830
Amps are basically a measure
of how much current, how

00:58:59.830 --> 00:59:02.410
much oomp, you're drawing
from that circuit.

00:59:02.410 --> 00:59:06.850
And the wattage of a
lamp, 2,000, 100 watts,

00:59:06.850 --> 00:59:11.403
is how much energy that
lamp is putting to work.

00:59:11.403 --> 00:59:12.820
So the important
thing is that you

00:59:12.820 --> 00:59:15.070
don't want to draw more
current from the circuit

00:59:15.070 --> 00:59:18.200
than it can take because then
you'll pop the circuit breaker.

00:59:18.200 --> 00:59:22.090
So whenever you set
up a lighting kit,

00:59:22.090 --> 00:59:27.100
if your total lighting
kit only uses 1,500 watts,

00:59:27.100 --> 00:59:29.110
you probably don't have
to worry about popping

00:59:29.110 --> 00:59:30.250
an ordinary breaker.

00:59:30.250 --> 00:59:33.790
But you want to know where the
breaker box is just in case.

00:59:33.790 --> 00:59:35.440
And whenever I go
into somebody's home

00:59:35.440 --> 00:59:38.300
to do an interview or
whatever, I always ask them,

00:59:38.300 --> 00:59:42.340
do you have anything plugged
in that if the power went out

00:59:42.340 --> 00:59:44.020
would cause some of disaster?

00:59:44.020 --> 00:59:46.150
Always ask.

00:59:46.150 --> 00:59:47.680
There are times,
for example, when

00:59:47.680 --> 00:59:50.020
I've run extension
cords into a kitchen

00:59:50.020 --> 00:59:52.630
to power an interview
in a living room

00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:54.550
because somebody had
their computer set up

00:59:54.550 --> 00:59:56.890
and they didn't want to
turn it off and whatever.

00:59:56.890 --> 00:59:58.390
You just don't want
to mess with it.

00:59:58.390 --> 01:00:00.910
But the thing is, in
modern construction,

01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:03.700
you can always count on
a solid 20 amp circuit

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:06.460
in a kitchen or a bathroom.

01:00:06.460 --> 01:00:10.340
And usually, there's nothing
else plugged into that circuit.

01:00:10.340 --> 01:00:14.290
So this will go into some
safety tips, common sense,

01:00:14.290 --> 01:00:16.030
that you should read.

01:00:16.030 --> 01:00:20.140
And I mentioned a good
resource for further study

01:00:20.140 --> 01:00:24.670
is Ross Lowell's wonderful book
Matters of Light and Depth.

01:00:24.670 --> 01:00:27.880
To date, it's the best book
ever written about lighting.

01:00:27.880 --> 01:00:31.240
It's written from the point
of view a lot of experience.

01:00:31.240 --> 01:00:33.280
And what's interesting
about Ross Lowell

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:37.990
is he has done a lot of shooting
with a minimal amount of gear.

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:41.230
And it's written from the point
of view of getting the most out

01:00:41.230 --> 01:00:44.140
of the least amount of
gear in terms of lighting.

01:00:44.140 --> 01:00:48.220
And Ross Lowell developed
a company, Lowel-Lights,

01:00:48.220 --> 01:00:49.780
to sell his--

01:00:49.780 --> 01:00:52.420
at the time, he developed
a whole new generation

01:00:52.420 --> 01:00:53.950
of lightweight
lighting units, which

01:00:53.950 --> 01:00:56.200
were very popular
among documentarians

01:00:56.200 --> 01:00:58.540
who didn't want to carry
around big equipment cases.

01:00:58.540 --> 01:01:01.060
They wanted to carry around
small equipment cases.

01:01:01.060 --> 01:01:04.780
And it's also written with
a wonderful sense of humor.

01:01:04.780 --> 01:01:07.030
You will chuckle while
you read this book.

01:01:07.030 --> 01:01:10.480
So Matters of Light and
Depth, highly recommended.

01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:14.800
And there's also a lot of
wonderful resources online.

01:01:14.800 --> 01:01:18.820
dvinfo.net is a wonderful
discussion group for matters

01:01:18.820 --> 01:01:20.470
of a technical nature.

01:01:20.470 --> 01:01:24.760
And selfreliantfilm.com is a
wonderful blog about making

01:01:24.760 --> 01:01:30.910
films do it yourself style,
which I highly recommend.

01:01:30.910 --> 01:01:32.680
And that's it.

01:01:32.680 --> 01:01:35.470
PROFESSOR: So just to
wrap up, the second half

01:01:35.470 --> 01:01:38.080
after a short break is
going to be the demo.

01:01:38.080 --> 01:01:40.870
And a lot of the things that
David talked about and showed

01:01:40.870 --> 01:01:44.440
in the PowerPoint will
become a little bit clearer

01:01:44.440 --> 01:01:45.940
as we go through the demo.

01:01:45.940 --> 01:01:49.400
But just to wrap up,
a lot of lighting,

01:01:49.400 --> 01:01:53.680
especially lighting on our
level, beginning and low,

01:01:53.680 --> 01:01:56.200
no instrument
lighting, boils down

01:01:56.200 --> 01:01:58.360
to just learning
how to see things.

01:01:58.360 --> 01:02:00.925
And it's so much
like other things

01:02:00.925 --> 01:02:04.210
in shooting, framing, learning
how to shoot for an edit.

01:02:04.210 --> 01:02:07.990
You really need to practice
seeing what's actually there

01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:11.140
and thinking about how that is
going to affect your audience.

01:02:11.140 --> 01:02:12.820
You're the first
audience for your film.

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:15.370
So all of the things that
David talked about at the end

01:02:15.370 --> 01:02:19.090
really boil down to
seeing what's there

01:02:19.090 --> 01:02:21.310
and learning how to make
a determination about what

01:02:21.310 --> 01:02:24.040
needs to be changed in order
to get the image that's

01:02:24.040 --> 01:02:26.950
going to have the impact
that it needs to have.

01:02:26.950 --> 01:02:30.820
So often, and I think I've
said this before, but so often,

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:33.940
even people who have been
doing film for a long time

01:02:33.940 --> 01:02:35.920
sometimes fall into
the trap of forgetting

01:02:35.920 --> 01:02:38.980
that you can actually make a
change because it's difficult

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:42.850
or it's imposition is
problematic for somebody.

01:02:42.850 --> 01:02:46.510
But if it really is necessary
to make some kind of change,

01:02:46.510 --> 01:02:48.230
it's really up to you to do it.

01:02:48.230 --> 01:02:51.315
So if you're shooting in a room,
doing an interview in a room

01:02:51.315 --> 01:02:52.690
or in a location
that's not going

01:02:52.690 --> 01:02:56.740
to work because the lighting
just is impossible to do,

01:02:56.740 --> 01:02:58.660
if the person standing
in a place that is just

01:02:58.660 --> 01:03:02.060
going to be a disaster,
you need to make a change.

01:03:02.060 --> 01:03:04.610
You need to ask them
to move somewhere else.

01:03:04.610 --> 01:03:10.270
And using your experience
and your sensitivity

01:03:10.270 --> 01:03:12.910
to what the situation is based
on some of these things David's

01:03:12.910 --> 01:03:14.560
talked about, these
basic principles,

01:03:14.560 --> 01:03:18.610
then determines where
you get them to move to,

01:03:18.610 --> 01:03:20.950
what change you actually
affect in order to get

01:03:20.950 --> 01:03:23.050
the image that you need to get.

01:03:23.050 --> 01:03:23.733
So that's it.

01:03:23.733 --> 01:03:25.150
I think the second
part of this is

01:03:25.150 --> 01:03:27.400
going to be really fun because
we're going to see some

01:03:27.400 --> 01:03:29.050
of these principles in action.

01:03:29.050 --> 01:03:31.410
So thanks a lot.

